Instructions:
1. Write a compelling, high-retention Hook (the first 10-15 seconds) to grab the viewer's attention and set a clear curiosity gap.
2. Break down the body into chronological sections. For each section, provide:
   - Title: The name of the section.
   - Text: Spoken narration. Make it engaging, using conversational transitions, rhetorical questions, and clear examples. Keep it natural (no brackets/stage directions in this field).
   - Visual Cues: Specify when B-roll should play, where text overlays should appear, or when to cut/zoom the camera.
   - Audio Cues: Specify background music shifts (e.g., "music stops for emphasis", "upbeat transition track") and sound effects (SFX) such as whooshes, typing sounds, or bell chimes.
3. Write a natural and persuasive Call to Action (CTA) and a brief Outro.
4. Maintain a speaking pace of ~150 words per minute. Aim for approximately {{ (duration_minutes * 150) | int }} total words in the script.
